messages:
lee-docs: Quick note from onboarding: our README JS quickstart still uses `client.generateSync(...)` in the code block. The latest JS SDK removed sync APIs and everything is async/Promise-based. New users are copy/pasting, running node and seeing nothing (or unhandled promise warnings). We've had two support tickets this week.

ben-js: Ah ugh, relic from the pre-async refactor. The sync samples were convenient to show flow but they no longer work. We should swap to `await client.generate(...)` and show a minimal top-level async wrapper or use top-level await for node >=16.8.

tara-support: The tickets are basically: "example doesn't return anything" and "process exits before response". Customers pasted the snippet into their CI and assumed the SDK was broken. We lost a few minutes diagnosing.

sam-devex: Proposed quick fixes: 1) Replace sync example with an async example using top-level await and include a one-line note for older Node versions. 2) Add a small runnable example in the repo that CI can run (smoke example). 3) Add a short paragraph about retries: our SDK defaults to an exponential backoff but the plain curl example in READMEs shows a naive `while` retry which caused duplicate requests to certain idempotency-sensitive endpoints.

ben-js: For the READMEs I suggest two variants: a minimal snippet for copy/paste that uses top-level await, and a fuller snippet that shows proper error handling and `maxRetries` configuration. Example minimal: 
```js
// Node 18+ (top-level await)
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY });
const res = await client.generate({ model: 'rwd-small', prompt: 'Hello' });
console.log(res.text);
```
Full example for older Node: 
```js
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY, maxRetries: 3 });
async function main() {
  try {
    const res = await client.generate({ model: 'rwd-small', prompt: 'Hello' });
    console.log(res.text);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('request failed', e);
    process.exit(1);
  }
}
main();
```

andre-qa: Also flagging the Go quickstart: we show a naive retry loop in the README that re-submits the same request without idempotency keys. For POST endpoints that mutate server-side state (some customers use internal rerank endpoints), that could cause duplicates. Suggest we show a retry example that uses a unique request ID header or use the SDK's built-in retry helper: `client.WithIdempotencyKey(...)`.

ci-bot: Note: I created issue #1298 for adding a docs smoke test to run the example snippets in a minimal container. CI will execute the repo's `examples/quickstart/*` and fail the docs deploy if they diverge.

sam-devex: I'll open the PR to update the README and add the runnable example. Also adding a small test that asserts the JS snippet includes either `await` or `top-level await` and that the Go example uses `Idempotency` headers.

ben-js: PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/501 (JS sync->async fix, Go idempotency note, smoke test). I've put a changelog entry under docs: "fix: README JS sample uses async API; add retry/idempotency guidance".

tara-support: Sweet. Once merged can we post a short note in #onboarding and update the quickstart template in the CLI generator? That template still injects the old sync snippet.

sam-devex: Good catch — I'll update the CLI generator template too. CI will test the generated example against Node 18 in the matrix.

lee-docs: Thanks folks. This should prevent the next wave of confusion.

andre-qa: Left a small QA checklist in the PR for reviewers to verify snippets actually run.

ci-bot: Linked issue: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/issues/1298 assigned to @sam-devex.
